<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="d56937bb">&lt;p&gt;
    The case for change receives a fair amount of focus early on in an initiative; however it is valuable for
    communications and use throughout the change program lifecycle as a reminder of why the project or program exists, and
    why people are asked to change.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This work product should be completed through collaborative sessions with client stakeholders and the project
    or program team so that it covers all relevant areas and positions the available information in the most compelling
    way.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Research and benchmarking exercises can assist in supporting the case for change.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="7ac490d3"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="ce7ab086"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="a6467cbf">&lt;p&gt;
    A compelling case for change, or “burning platform”, is a critical requirement when communicating with people about the
    rationale for change and gaining their acceptance and, if possible, their commitment to the project or program.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
